In terms of color patterns and markings, the salamander typically has a light-colored base coat, often pale yellow or cream, adorned with darker spots. These spots can range from dark brown to black and are distributed irregularly along the body, creating a distinctive mottled appearance. The underside of the salamander is usually lighter than the top side, sometimes appearing almost white.

The size and proportions of the spotted salamander are moderate; it is not particularly large, with an average total length ranging from about 6 to 8 inches (15 to 20 cm). The proportions are balanced, with a slender yet robust build.

