MLLM description: The leatherback turtle is characterized by its large, streamlined body with a broad, flat carapace (upper shell) and a relatively small head and neck. Its flippers are paddle-shaped, which aid in swimming through the water. The skin is smooth and dark-colored, often appearing almost black or dark gray, with a glossy sheen. The shell has a leathery texture, hence the name "leatherback." The coloration can vary, but it typically includes shades of brown, olive, or black, sometimes with lighter patches on the underside. The leatherback turtle is the largest sea turtle species, with adults reaching lengths up to 6.7 feet (2 meters) and weights over 2,000 pounds (907 kg). They have a unique ability to dive deep into the ocean, reaching depths of up to 4,000 feet (1,219 meters), which allows them to feed on deep-sea jellyfish. Their distinctive features include a lack of a bony plastron (lower shell), a more elongated shape compared to other turtles, and a more pronounced keel on the lower part of their carapace.
