MLLM description: The Windsor tie is characterized by its distinctive physical features, including a wide knot that sits higher on the chest compared to other tie knots like the Four-in-hand or Half-Windsor. This knot is formed by crossing the wider end of the tie over the narrower end, then looping it under and over itself before tying a knot. The distinctive shape of the knot gives the Windsor tie its name.

In terms of color patterns and markings, the Windsor tie in the images appears to have a striped pattern with alternating dark and light stripes. The stripes are consistent across the length of the tie, creating a uniform appearance. The size and proportions of the tie are relatively standard, with a width of approximately 3 inches and a length of around 60 inches when knotted. 

Behaviorally, the Windsor tie is often associated with formal occasions and business attire due to its classic and sophisticated look. It is typically worn with a suit and white dress shirt, as seen in the first two images. The unique identifying trait of the Windsor tie is its knot style, which sets it apart from other tie knots and contributes to its distinct appearance.
